{
  "dom_id": "step-q325351",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.$\n\n$y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(5,0),\\,a=\\sqrt{9}=3,\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\pm (\\frac{4}{3})(x-5) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\,\\frac{4}{3}(x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
